1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,955
The skin on your hand.

2
00:00:01,955 --> 00:00:03,597
The inside of your cheek.

3
00:00:03,597 --> 00:00:05,330
The lining of your stomach.

4
00:00:05,730 --> 00:00:09,804
Each one is a covering, a sheet
of cells with one side facing out.

5
00:00:10,254 --> 00:00:12,741
Diya scraped her knee and watched it heal.

6
00:00:12,741 --> 00:00:17,002
She wants to be a doctor, so she
asked: what is this covering made of?

7
00:00:17,002 --> 00:00:20,587
And why is skin thick, but the
lining of the lungs so thin?

8
00:00:20,937 --> 00:00:22,572
One tissue answers both.

9
00:00:22,572 --> 00:00:24,691
Epithelium, the covering tissue.

10
00:00:24,691 --> 00:00:28,281
Its cell shape and number of
layers change with its job.

11
00:00:28,781 --> 00:00:33,660
By the end, you will know simple and
compound epithelium, squamous, cuboidal

12
00:00:33,660 --> 00:00:36,885
and columnar cells,
glands, and cell junctions.

13
00:00:37,535 --> 00:00:39,157
First, what is a tissue?

14
00:00:39,508 --> 00:00:43,768
A tissue is a group of similar
cells working together for one job.

15
00:00:43,768 --> 00:00:48,589
Animals have four basic kinds: epithelial,
connective, muscular and neural.

16
00:00:49,039 --> 00:00:51,379
Epithelial tissue covers and lines.

17
00:00:51,379 --> 00:00:55,418
It has a free surface, facing
a body fluid or the outside.

18
00:00:55,418 --> 00:00:59,855
Its cells are packed tight, with
very little material between them.

19
00:01:00,505 --> 00:01:02,777
Let's zoom into the small intestine.

20
00:01:03,127 --> 00:01:07,437
In the body, the intestine is a
long, coiled tube that soaks up food.

21
00:01:07,937 --> 00:01:09,267
Cut open its wall.

22
00:01:09,267 --> 00:01:12,959
The inside surface is covered
by one smooth sheet of cells.

23
00:01:13,459 --> 00:01:15,113
Zoom in on that sheet.

24
00:01:15,113 --> 00:01:19,653
Tall, slender cells stand side by
side, each with its nucleus near the

25
00:01:19,653 --> 00:01:20,553
base.

26
00:01:20,215 --> 00:01:22,109
This is columnar epithelium.

27
00:01:22,609 --> 00:01:26,624
Their free surface has microvilli,
tiny finger-like folds that give more

28
00:01:26,624 --> 00:01:27,997
area to absorb food.

29
00:01:27,997 --> 00:01:30,695
Here and there, a goblet cell makes mucus.

30
00:01:31,345 --> 00:01:34,776
Now let's sort simple
epithelium, step by step.

31
00:01:35,126 --> 00:01:36,026
Step one.

32
00:01:35,882 --> 00:01:38,627
Simple epithelium is a
single layer of cells.

33
00:01:38,627 --> 00:01:41,179
It lines body cavities, ducts and tubes.

34
00:01:41,929 --> 00:01:42,829
Step two.

35
00:01:42,715 --> 00:01:47,188
Squamous cells are thin and flat,
with uneven edges, like floor tiles.

36
00:01:47,188 --> 00:01:51,691
They form the walls of blood vessels
and the air sacs of the lungs, where

37
00:01:51,691 --> 00:01:53,750
things must pass through easily.

38
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:55,400
Step three.

39
00:01:55,367 --> 00:01:57,240
Cuboidal cells are cube-shaped.

40
00:01:57,240 --> 00:02:01,525
They line the ducts of glands and
the tubes of the kidney, and help in

41
00:02:01,525 --> 00:02:03,107
secretion and absorption.

42
00:02:03,857 --> 00:02:04,757
Step four.

43
00:02:04,689 --> 00:02:07,886
Columnar cells are tall,
with nuclei at the base.

44
00:02:07,886 --> 00:02:10,442
They line the stomach and the intestine.

45
00:02:11,192 --> 00:02:12,092
Step five.

46
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:15,907
When cuboidal or columnar cells carry
cilia, tiny beating hairs, it is

47
00:02:15,907 --> 00:02:17,116
ciliated epithelium.

48
00:02:17,116 --> 00:02:21,346
It moves mucus or particles one way,
as in the bronchioles and the fallopian

49
00:02:21,346 --> 00:02:22,246
tubes.

50
00:02:22,634 --> 00:02:23,679
Remember this.

51
00:02:23,679 --> 00:02:26,588
Simple epithelium is a
single layer of cells.

52
00:02:27,188 --> 00:02:30,358
Some epithelial cells
make and release things.

53
00:02:30,358 --> 00:02:32,429
This is glandular epithelium.

54
00:02:32,779 --> 00:02:37,503
A gland of one cell is unicellular,
like the goblet cell in the gut.

55
00:02:37,503 --> 00:02:41,836
A gland of many cells is
multicellular, like the salivary gland.

56
00:02:42,286 --> 00:02:46,062
Exocrine glands pour their product
through a tube called a duct.

57
00:02:46,062 --> 00:02:50,719
Mucus, saliva, earwax, oil, milk and
digestive enzymes all leave this way.

58
00:02:51,169 --> 00:02:52,881
Endocrine glands have no duct.

59
00:02:52,881 --> 00:02:56,221
They release hormones straight
into the fluid around the gland.

60
00:02:56,871 --> 00:02:59,998
Now, cells in a sheet must
hold on to each other.

61
00:02:59,998 --> 00:03:01,526
They use cell junctions.

62
00:03:01,876 --> 00:03:06,558
Tight junctions seal the gap between
cells, so substances cannot leak across

63
00:03:06,558 --> 00:03:07,480
the tissue.

64
00:03:07,930 --> 00:03:10,096
Adhering junctions work like cement.

65
00:03:10,096 --> 00:03:12,685
They keep neighbouring
cells stuck together.

66
00:03:13,135 --> 00:03:17,379
Gap junctions are tiny tunnels joining
the cytoplasm of two cells, so ions

67
00:03:17,379 --> 00:03:20,918
and small molecules pass quickly
from one cell to the next.

68
00:03:21,568 --> 00:03:24,858
Now the big pair: simple
and compound epithelium.

69
00:03:25,208 --> 00:03:26,854
Simple: one thin layer.

70
00:03:26,854 --> 00:03:31,433
Good for passing things through, like
gases in the air sacs of the lungs.

71
00:03:31,883 --> 00:03:34,041
Compound: two or more layers.

72
00:03:34,041 --> 00:03:35,994
Its main job is protection.

73
00:03:35,994 --> 00:03:40,711
It covers the dry skin, and lines
the moist mouth and pharynx, and the

74
00:03:40,711 --> 00:03:43,557
ducts of salivary glands and the pancreas.

75
00:03:44,157 --> 00:03:45,057
Your turn.

76
00:03:44,981 --> 00:03:48,353
The inside of your cheek
gets rubbed by food all day.

77
00:03:48,353 --> 00:03:50,237
Simple or compound epithelium?

78
00:03:50,237 --> 00:03:51,449
Pause and decide.

79
00:03:54,649 --> 00:03:55,549
Compound!

80
00:03:55,412 --> 00:04:00,146
The lining of the mouth, the buccal
cavity, is compound epithelium, built

81
00:04:00,146 --> 00:04:01,892
in layers for protection.

82
00:04:02,542 --> 00:04:04,039
Now, a worked example.

83
00:04:04,039 --> 00:04:04,987
Three linings.

84
00:04:04,987 --> 00:04:06,263
Let's name each one.

85
00:04:06,613 --> 00:04:09,437
First, the wall of a tiny blood vessel.

86
00:04:09,437 --> 00:04:11,214
One layer of flat cells.

87
00:04:11,214 --> 00:04:12,402
Simple squamous.

88
00:04:12,852 --> 00:04:14,978
Second, a tube of the kidney.

89
00:04:14,978 --> 00:04:16,995
One layer of cube-shaped cells.

90
00:04:16,995 --> 00:04:18,146
Simple cuboidal.

91
00:04:18,596 --> 00:04:21,056
Third, the windpipe
branches, the bronchioles.

92
00:04:21,056 --> 00:04:22,677
Tall cells with beating hairs.

93
00:04:23,127 --> 00:04:25,071
That is ciliated epithelium.

94
00:04:25,071 --> 00:04:28,568
Its cilia sweep mucus and
dust away from the lungs.

95
00:04:29,218 --> 00:04:30,680
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:31,030 --> 00:04:35,355
Many students think skin is simple
epithelium, because it is a covering.

97
00:04:35,755 --> 00:04:40,385
But skin is compound epithelium,
many layers thick, for protection.

98
00:04:40,385 --> 00:04:42,921
Simple epithelium is only one layer.

99
00:04:43,571 --> 00:04:44,876
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:44,876 --> 00:04:48,369
As match-the-following questions
pairing each epithelium with where it

101
00:04:48,369 --> 00:04:49,269
is found.

102
00:04:49,687 --> 00:04:50,849
Exam level now.

103
00:04:50,849 --> 00:04:53,783
Match each epithelium
with where it is found.

104
00:04:53,783 --> 00:04:54,995
Pause and solve.

105
00:05:00,395 --> 00:05:01,863
The answer is C.

106
00:05:01,863 --> 00:05:04,244
One-r, two-s, three-p, four-q.

107
00:05:04,744 --> 00:05:07,445
Squamous lines the air sacs of the lungs.

108
00:05:07,445 --> 00:05:09,991
Cuboidal lines the tubes of the kidney.

109
00:05:10,491 --> 00:05:12,767
Ciliated lines the fallopian tubes.

110
00:05:12,767 --> 00:05:15,202
And compound lines the buccal cavity.

111
00:05:15,802 --> 00:05:17,342
Here is your recap.

112
00:05:17,342 --> 00:05:18,925
Screenshot this card.

113
00:05:22,125 --> 00:05:24,311
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

114
00:05:24,711 --> 00:05:25,611
One.

115
00:05:25,211 --> 00:05:27,871
Where is simple squamous epithelium found?

116
00:05:28,771 --> 00:05:29,671
Two.

117
00:05:29,245 --> 00:05:32,779
How is an exocrine gland
different from an endocrine gland?

118
00:05:33,679 --> 00:05:34,579
Three.

119
00:05:34,103 --> 00:05:36,639
Which cell junction lets two
cells share small molecules?

120
00:05:37,739 --> 00:05:40,984
Next time: bone, blood and
the muscle in your arm.

121
00:05:40,984 --> 00:05:42,821
How can they all be tissues?

122
00:05:42,821 --> 00:05:45,590
We find out in connective
and muscular tissue.
